arXiv:2510.04120v1 [cs.CL] 05 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Unveiling LLMs’ Metaphorical Understanding: Exploring Conceptual Irrelevance, Context Leveraging and Syntactic Influence</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Fengying Ye    
Shanshan Wang    
<span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lidia S. Chao</span>    
Derek F. Wong
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding Author</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author"><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>NLPCT Lab, Department of Computer and Information Science, University of Macaunlp2ct.{fengying,shanshan}@gmail.com, {derekfw,lidiasc}@um.edu.mo
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Metaphor analysis is a complex linguistic phenomenon shaped by context and external factors. While Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate advanced capabilities in knowledge integration, contextual reasoning, and creative generation, their mechanisms for metaphor comprehension remain insufficiently explored. This study examines LLMs’ metaphor-processing abilities from three perspectives: (1) Concept Mapping: using embedding space projections to evaluate how LLMs map concepts in target domains (e.g., misinterpreting “fall in love” as “drop down from love”); (2) Metaphor-Literal Repository: analyzing metaphorical words and their literal counterparts to identify inherent metaphorical knowledge; and (3) Syntactic Sensitivity: assessing how metaphorical syntactic structures influence LLMs’ performance. Our findings reveal that LLMs generate 15%-25% conceptually irrelevant interpretations, depend on metaphorical indicators in training data rather than contextual cues, and are more sensitive to syntactic irregularities than to structural comprehension. These insights underline the limitations of LLMs in metaphor analysis and call for more robust computational approaches.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04120v1/framework.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/196;" width="429" height="196" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The overview of experiment framework. Spatial analysis addresses the limitations of multi-choice task and multiple metaphorical mapping problem. For “trigger word” error, metaphorical imagination investigate context leveraging and the existence of a metaphor-literal repository within LLMs. Syntactic shuffle identifies the influence of syntax in metaphor analysis. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Metaphor is a pervasive and sophisticated aspect of human language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gibbs Jr (2008)</a></cite>. However, defining and analyzing metaphors poses significant challenges in both computational and traditional linguistics. Several linguistic frameworks have been proposed to approach metaphor analysis: Selection Preference Violation (SPV) and the Metaphor Identification Procedure (MIP), focus on detecting semantic deviations from conventional linguistic patterns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wilks (1975)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Group (2007)</a></cite>. However, Conceptual Metaphor Theory (CMT) views metaphors as cross-domain mappings between a source domain, which describes tangible objects, and a target domain, which represents abstract ideas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lakoff and Johnson (1980)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Croft (1993)</a></cite>. These domains indicate conceptual frameworks behind specific words that can also be implied in other expressions. Thus, <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a pair of source and target domains can encompass multiple metaphorical mappings</span>: The mapping “thinking is linguistic activity” can be the generic concept of “memorization is writing” or “ideas are words” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Do Dinh et al. (2018)</a></cite>. This uncertainty of mapping results in difficulty translating linguistic theories into computational procedures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With impressive capabilities in text comprehension and vast knowledge bases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, LLMs have been widely applied to metaphor computation on cultural contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ichien et al. (2024)</a></cite>, cross-lingual interpretation and detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. (2024)</a></cite> and genre-specific studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Toker et al. (2024)</a></cite>. However, recent studies highlight limitations in LLMs’ metaphor comprehension. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wachowiak and Gromann (2023)</a></cite> have discovered “<span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">trigger word</span>” error that hinders sufficient context analysis: LLMs predict wrong source domains that were not metaphorically related, because models only infer from the trigger word instead of considering context. (e.g., “The arms race.” is falsely identified as COMPETITION IS WAR, due to the commonly co-occurrence of “arm” and “war” in daily discourse). Also, the robustness of LLMs in multi-choice prompting of proper metaphor interpretation remains under debate–studies imply that this setting can be less effective due to LLMs’ hallucination <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2021)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is crucial to investigate LLMs’ inherent metaphor understanding for future enhancement. This study primarily focuses on three perspectives of LLMs in metaphorical analysis. 1) We explore the <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">concept-irrelevant error</span>: the interpretation generated by LLM failed to clarify the actual target domain that source domain intended to map (e.g., interpreting <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">falling in love</span> as <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">physically falling</span> rather than <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">entering an emotional state</span>). 2) Extend from the “<span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">trigger word</span>” scenario, we investigate the inherent metaphor-literal repository of LLMs that indicates limited <span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">leveraging of context</span> in metaphor analysis and 3) examine how <span id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">syntactic structures</span> influence metaphor detection. Our contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are the first to investigate concept-irrelevant error in metaphor interpretation from high-dimensional space projections derived through dimensionality reduction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We discuss the existence of a metaphor-literal repository within LLMs by analyzing contextualized and de-contextualized imagination, demonstrating how context and genre influence metaphor analysis.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We explore the syntactic structure impact: random word order, part-of-speech (POS) and positional placement of metaphorical words on LLMs’ metaphor analysis.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We reveal that LLMs can produce concept-irrelevant error in metaphor interpretation that may relate to limited ability to leverage contextual and syntactic information.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">In Appendix <a href="#S7.SS1" title="7.1 Definition and Terminology ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ Unveiling LLMs’ Metaphorical Understanding: Exploring Conceptual Irrelevance, Context Leveraging and Syntactic Influence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>, we present a detailed compilation of terminologies and definitions in the paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Metaphor Detection</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Metaphor detection involves the computational task of identifying whether a given input contains metaphorical expressions. Early approaches relied on rule-based linguistic frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Group (2007)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dodge et al. (2015)</a></cite>. With the advent of neural networks, metaphor detection has reformulated as a classification task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rai and Chakraverty (2020)</a></cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al. (2020)</a></cite> incorporated linguistic conceptual norms into machine learning algorithms, which leverage word norms, the informal associations between closely connected words, such as <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">nurse and doctor</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Church and Hanks (1990)</a></cite>. These norms are influenced by factors such as language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Montefinese et al. (2014)</a></cite>, genre <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brysbaert and New (2009)</a></cite>, social context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheung et al. (2009)</a></cite> and cultural background <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Janschewitz (2008)</a></cite>, leading to domain-specific variations in metaphor detection. Pretrained language models encode metaphorical knowledge within representations and have demonstrated promising results in various tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mao et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ge et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choi et al. (2021)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Metaphor Interpretation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Metaphor interpretation focuses on unveiling metaphorical mappings and their associated conceptual domains. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sullivan (2013)</a></cite> highlights the constructions of metaphors and their reliance on cross-domain mappings. A key computational method of metaphor interpretation is metaphor component recognition, which identifies and analyzes the source and target domains of metaphors. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sengupta et al. (2024)</a></cite> developed a dataset annotated with conceptual mappings from news editorials to examine political framing. Similarly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ge et al. (2022)</a></cite> proposed a concept projection model designed for metaphor detection and component recognition. In pretrained models, frameworks such as Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning combined with knowledge graphs have been introduced to improve metaphor interpretation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Another approach involves explaining metaphors with literal expressions. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mao et al. (2024)</a></cite> implemented contrastive learning based on SPV, though challenges remain in cross-lingual metaphor interpretation and long-text analysis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Metaphor in LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Research in metaphor processing with LLMs has produced exciting findings. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jie Wang and Zhang (2024)</a></cite> proposed a multi-stage generative heuristic-enhanced prompt framework to introduce conceptual background for Chinese metaphor interpretation. Studies also have explored LLMs’ associative capabilities to investigate creativity and novelty in metaphor generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">DiStefano et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al. (2025)</a></cite>. For metaphor detection, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a></cite> applied CoT reasoning to improve LLMs’ performance in multi-modal settings. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aghazadeh et al. (2022)</a></cite> discovered that pretrained models encode metaphorical knowledge within their representations, providing a basis for embedding-level analysis. Even without explicit fine-tuning, LLMs have demonstrated some ability in cross-lingual metaphor detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wachowiak and Gromann (2023)</a></cite>. However, current research lacks standardized evaluation metrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ge et al. (2023)</a></cite>, making it difficult to quantify LLMs’ performance across varying aspects of metaphors. Therefore, further studies are needed to investigate LLMs’ metaphor-processing capabilities for future refinements.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments are conducted at both interpretation and detection levels to explore LLMs’ metaphorical understanding. The overall experiment framework is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Unveiling LLMs’ Metaphorical Understanding: Exploring Conceptual Irrelevance, Context Leveraging and Syntactic Influence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interpretation Level:</span> While multi-choice experiments in metaphor interpretation can be affected by the hallucination, <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">multiple metaphorical mappings across domains</span> (e.g., <span id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“thinking is linguistic activity”</span> can be the generic concept of <span id="S3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“memorization is writing”</span> or <span id="S3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“ideas are words”</span>) can also complicate metaphor analysis, which highlights the necessity of analyzing metaphors at conceptual level. Thus, we introduce the <span id="S3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">spatial analysis</span> framework to evaluate conceptual irrelevance in metaphor interpretation. This approach maps LLM-generated interpretations into a high-dimensional embedding space, quantifying concept-irrelevant information of the generated outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detection Level:</span> Building on trigger word error identified by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wachowiak and Gromann (2023)</a></cite> and the impact of irregularity in syntactic structure on metaphor detection, we propose two complementary experiments. <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metaphor Imagination</span>: LLMs are tasked to imagine corresponding literal or metaphorical lexical sets both in context and without context. By comparing the overlap among different settings, we can investigate the influence of context in metaphor analysis. A high overlap implies LLMs inherently connect metaphorical words with commonly co-occurred literal expression–metaphor-literal repository that result in metaphor detection based on specific words regardless of context. <span id="S3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Syntactic Shuffle</span>: We disrupt the semantic coherence and syntax of metaphor to investigate whether syntactic structures influence metaphor detection. This experiment tests whether LLMs rely on syntactic information for metaphor analysis.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04120v1/spacial.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/201;" width="405" height="201"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Illustration of spatial analysis in three-dimensional space. <math id="S3.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is the perpendicular distance from <math id="S3.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to the conceptual plane. <math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{oi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{oi}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the distance from <math id="S3.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04120v1/cos1.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/145;" width="357" height="145"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2510.04120v1/cos2.png" id="S3.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/149;" width="357" height="149" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Illustration of angle <math id="S3.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> between the conceptual plane and the interpretation plane. The conceptual plane is defined by the representations of <math id="S3.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, while the interpretation plane is defined by <math id="S3.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Spatial Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Definition </span>Inspired by similarity-based framework proposed in style evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wegmann and Nguyen (2021)</a></cite>, we extend the concept-irrelevance, the comparison of included conceptual mappings between human interpretation of the metaphor and LLM-generated interpretation from two-dimensional to high-dimensional space, analyzing the geometric relationships between sentences. We treat LLM-generated interpretations of a metaphor <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as representation <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and compare it with the representation of actual concept mapping to the metaphor: 1) We embed {<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>} (human interpretations) and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> (LLM-generated equivalent interpretation) to collectively construct a <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">conceptual plane</span> <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>–a semantic space delineates the conceptual information in the three sentences, representing the ideal concept mapping the metaphor intended to convey. 2) We measure the projection and discrepancy between <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> interpreted by LLMs and the conceptual plane <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to reveal the corresponding concept-irrelevant error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Methodology</span> As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Unveiling LLMs’ Metaphorical Understanding: Exploring Conceptual Irrelevance, Context Leveraging and Syntactic Influence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Unveiling LLMs’ Metaphorical Understanding: Exploring Conceptual Irrelevance, Context Leveraging and Syntactic Influence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, two measures are defined in the spatial analysis:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">1. <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>: The perpendicular distance from generated outputs to the conceptual plane <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating conceptual irrelevance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">2. <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\theta</annotation></semantics></math>: The cosine similarity between conceptual plane <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and interpretation plane <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by LLM-generated interpretation <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the two human-annotated interpretations {<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>}. Representing the concept-irrelevant offset compared with standard conceptual plane.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> measures the distance from <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to the conceptual plane defined by {<math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>}, capturing sentence-plane differences. Meanwhile, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\theta</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies inter-planar divergence: Ideal alignment is achieved when <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos\theta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\theta=1</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating negligible irrelevant information in the linguistic encoding. Together, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\theta</annotation></semantics></math> provide complementary perspectives for concept-irrelevant information in generated interpretations.</p>
</div>
